Z||000000||Spyro86SE||07-10-2001||04:55 PM||||just wondering if any one here is any good with changing ball joints i never done them before so not to sure of whats involved with changing them so if any one in the chicagoland area that would be willing to help it would be greatly appreciated  almost forgot i need to change my motor and trans mounts too <P>i'll buy the pizza and beer <P>thanks in advance<BR>mikey<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spryologo_1_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||07-10-2001||05:02 PM||||Are you replacing all of them or just the upper and lower front?  <P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||07-10-2001||06:44 PM||||Upper and lower front are quite easy.  I've done them before and will be doing them again tomorrow night (need one upper and one lower on front right).  What I recommend you do is get a ball joint separating tool (like a fork).  You can usually rent them for free at the place that sells you the ball joints (that's what I'm doing).  I've also done it by hammering the old ones out of the hub assembly (a bit tougher and you have to be careful not to damage anything).  Anyways, the job's not too hard.  If I remember correctly, first you should remove the brake calipers (not sure if it's necessary but certainly makes access easier).  Then, if you have the original joint (top), you may have to drill out the rivets.  I believe the after market ones come with bolts/nuts.  Then separate the joint from the hub.  It's a bit different on the bottom 'cause there is only one main bolt so nothing to drill out.  I don't quite remember the best order of things (bottom or top first) but I think separating the bottom first will make it easier.  One thing you should do if you don't want to get an alignment afterwards, is mark (scrape) around the joint assembly (on the control arm) with something sharp so you can install the new ones in the same position.  That's not fool proof 'cause the after market ones are sometimes a bit different dimensionally but do it anyways.  Some people will tell you to get an alignment afterwards regardless - your choice (I didn't get an alignment the first time around and the tires wore quite evenly).<BR>You can more or less figure this out as you go - it's not that hard.<BR>Oh, and I've never done the rear ones but I believe it's very similar.<BR>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||07-10-2001||07:49 PM||||I found that if you smack the spindle right where the taper on the balljoint is,sideways,nice and square,that they'll often pop right out.<BR>Doing this creates a shock wave inside the taper.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-10-2001||08:52 PM||||I have changed front and rear ball joints before. Not too hard to do, and I have the tools. I've even done the engine/trans mounts without dropping the cradle recently. As for Pizza and Beer, I was thinking more like Steak and Mike's Hard Lemonade.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>We could get a few folks to work together on all the stuff. Just remember Haze has to supervise, I get to pick out the steaks, and Leadfoot gets to pick out the quantity of the hard lemonade!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
